WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.440
Mind-melting theories about the Joker are
a dime a dozen.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.400
When the late, great Heath Ledger slipped
into the perma-grinning psychopath's purple

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.370
suit in The Dark Knight, fans went wild with
theories, and one stood out in particular

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.680
— that Ledger's Joker is actually a war
veteran.

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:20.220
The idea was sparked by the scene in which
the character marches in a police parade without

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.440
rocking his signature made-up face.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:27.660
Comedian and noted geek Patton Oswalt is a
fan who's in favor of this idea, but he recently

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.520
shared his own hypothesis about Heath Ledger's
Joker that builds off the "war veteran" theory.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:37.360
Oswalt suggested in a lengthy Facebook post
that The Clown Prince of Crime isn't just

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:43.320
an ex-military man, he's also "ex-military
intelligence" — specifically interrogation.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.080
After re-watching The Dark Knight, Oswalt
realized,

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:51.560
"[The Joker] seems to be very good at the
kind of mind-f---ery that sustained, professional

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.180
interrogation requires."

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:54.720
After pointing out that...

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.860
"'[Ledger's Joker] adjusts his personality
and methods depending on who he's talking

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.600
to, and knowing EXACTLY the reaction he'll
get."

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.280
Oswalt supported his claim by noting specific
moves the villain makes, like poking fun at

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:12.030
mob boss Gambol, digging into James Gordon's
isolation, appealing to Harvey Dent's, quote,

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.660
"hunger for fairness" and…

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.409
"[...] Invoking terror to Brian, the “false”
Batman; teasing the policeman’s sense of

00:01:18.409 --> 00:01:20.950
loyalty to his fallen, fellow cops."

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:24.550
Perhaps the most meaningful piece of the movie
Oswalt looked into is the moment when the

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:29.299
Joker conducts a, quote, "reverse interrogation"
on Batman while he's at the police station.

00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:33.829
The Joker wants to test how far he can push
Batman, and tries to coerce the Caped Crusader

00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:37.480
into breaking his "one rule" — his refusal
to take lives.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:42.160
A fellow fan noted in the comments that the
Joker "directs" Batman's interrogation of

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:43.600
him, as if he's teaching him.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.160
Oswalt added that the Joker, quote, "constantly
changes his backstory," and thus creates a

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.240
new identity with each lie as he panders to
the person he's speaking to.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.080
"To Gambol and his henchmen, he’s an abused
child (figuring that they were also products

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.380
of abuse and neglect).

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.420
To Rachel, he’s a man mourning a tragic
love — something she’s also wrestling

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:02.920
with."

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.440
By the end of The Dark Knight, Oswalt points
out,

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:10.460
"[The Joker] ends up trying to mind-f--- an
entire city and the city calls his bluff."

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.220
But that may have been what the criminal mastermind
wanted all along.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.040
When the Joker falls to his apparent doom,
he laughs like a child.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:23.470
When he's saved by Batman, the, quote, "one
individual he couldn't manipulate or break,

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:27.280
he's blissful and relieved" and turned upside
down visually.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.240
As Oswalt points out about the duo's final
scene,

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.380
"The language he uses when saying goodbye
to Batman describing their relationship as

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:40.360
an 'irresistible force meeting an immovable
object' is the kind of thing an interrogator

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.800
would say, ruefully, about a fruitless session."

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:47.600
Just as Oswalt's theory concludes, it apparently
doesn't really matter how the Joker got his

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:48.600
scars after all.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.540
And as far as any Dark Knight fan knows, the
Joker is still holed up somewhere in Arkham

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:57.840
Asylum, where he may or may not be crafting
new methods of interrogation.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:02:59.300
Thanks for watching!

